			<div class="content" id="post-content-4707267">
									

<p>I have a few things to recommend.</p>
<p><strong>Horror/Cosmic Horror Movies</strong></p>
<p><strong>The Void</strong> - It's probably one of the best portrayals of cosmic horror I've seen, tying back into a lot of tropes, like worshipping entities from other planes of existence. Some really good practical effects in this one.</p>
<p><strong>Annihilation</strong> - A great film that really shows just how alien life could be and how it might take forms we struggle to understand. As well as examining people and their own self-destructive behaviours. Great practical and cgi effects in this movie.</p>
<p><strong>Event Horizon</strong> - I can't say it's an amazing movie, but I think it does cosmic horror pretty well. As the crew investigate a ghost ship and uncover the truth or where it went. And how there are places we were never meant to go.</p>
<p><strong>Bird Box</strong> - This movie in my personal experience is hit or miss, depending solely on the person who watches it. Now I personally enjoyed it and thought it was a good portrayal of a horror concept, the monster you can't look at. Though some people I know, thought it was rubbish. It's an underrated gem in my books.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><strong>The Mist</strong> - This movie really does go full cosmic horror, with a mysterious force that sweeps in from another world. And the otherworldly creatures that it brings with it. The insanity of fanatics andreligious zealots and people just desperate for hope and survival in what seems to be, the end of the world.</p>
<p><strong>Virus</strong> - An energy being arrives onto our world, isolated within a Russian Navy vessel. And begins building machines, with an end goal of complete global conquest and the subjugation and annihilation of humanity. This is more of a B-Movie, not amazing, but I think the practical effects were great, particularly with all the large and small machines.</p>
<p><strong>Evil Dead (2013 film)</strong> - Despite not having Bruce Campbell, this movie is a solid standalone movie. I highly recommend this film, I think my only gripe would be with the Deadites in this version. Which I feel lack the psychological torment and mind games that they inflicted on Ash, during the Evil Dead trilogy.</p>
<p><strong>It Follows</strong> - I think this is an underrated gem, when it comes to horror movies, you know it's coming and it's never going to stop. That looming threat is seemingly inevitable, that you can never truly escape it.</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>Other Films</strong></p>
<p><strong>9 (2009 animated film)</strong> - This aniamted movie actually had a really good pre-release build up, I knew nothing about. There were all these snippets online, like of schematics for the war machines and excerpts of newspapers. I really recommend this film, it has some of the most creative designs I've seen for machine antagonists, you even get to see the main antagonist: The Fabrication Machine/B.R.A.I.N. (Binary Reactive Artificially Intelligent Neurocircuit) building them. The movie is a great post-apocalyptic setting, even if it isn't the prettiest animation around.</p>
<p><strong>The Black Cauldron</strong> - It's one of those Disney movies that really gets overlooked and seemingly forgotten by Disney itself. Which is a shame, because the Horned King is one of their most vile villains. And the story is overall interesting.</p>
<p><strong>Hugo</strong> - I find this film hard to describe, because when I start I generally gush about it and worry I'm going to spoil it for someone else. I'd just say give it a watch, I think you'll have a good time.</p>
<p><strong>Django Unchained</strong> - Tarantino's first foray into the western genre (To my knowledge) and one of his most entertaining films. Django doesn't shy away from the racism of the era, but also gives us a protagonist who uses his immense wit and linguistic skills to get himself out of most situations. I highly recommend this one.</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>Online series/webcomics</strong></p>
<p><strong>Look-See and others</strong> - Crypt TV's Look-See is a pretty unique monster, with an especially memorable design. I'd check out Crypt TV's various short films as they all seem to be in one big shared universe. Or at least a lot of them appear to be.</p>
<p><strong>Wonderlab</strong> - The comic based in the Lobotomy Corporation universe, I'd really recommend giving this a read.</p>
<p><strong>Property of Hate</strong> - A really fascinating universe, about a kid who's been asked to save a fantasy world and become a hero.</p>
<p><strong>Kill Six Billion Demons</strong> - A setting that's all about the multiverse and a young woman with the potential to change the established order. Really great worldbuilding in this one, with a fantastic art style.</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>Video games</strong></p>
<p><strong>Lobotomy Corporation</strong> - Want to feel like you're running a more profit oriented SCP-Foundation? Then Lobotomy Corporation is the game to play. An ideal time to play as the latest patch has corrected and fixed the english translation.</p>
<p><strong>Blasphemous</strong> - I helped kickstart this game and it is amazing, the pixel art is wonderfully detailed, multiple branching questlines. A bleak world in need of rescue from its own church. Worth playing if you enjoy Metroidvania type games.</p>
</div>
